        "id": 1132673
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-07-07T00:33:16Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Reading a sysprop is not an RPC. The properties having the same context are in a tree structure which is memory-mapped to the client. The APIs below are just accessor to the sysprop.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 66,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 67,
        "endChar": 68
      },
      "revId": "8b2ea14ea9e914347d0922850834e9c671bd518c",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6a6a40d1_d92efebb",
        "filename": "microdroid/keymint/service.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 67,
      "author": {
        "id": 1139038
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-07-12T14:11:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The normal accessor seems to copy the value into a std::string which then doesn\u0027t have any clean up once we\u0027re finished with it so we don\u0027t know where that piece of memory would end up, that was the bit I was trying to avoid.\n\nI\u0027m not sure what it means for properties to have the same context. Will all the processes have the secret mapped into their memory that they could access even if the SELinux rules say they can\u0027t? I assume that\u0027s not the case.",
      "parentUuid": "62a4a2f4_3a83b6af",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 66,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 67,
        "endChar": 68
      },
      "revId": "8b2ea14ea9e914347d0922850834e9c671bd518c",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f77fc38f_66987368",
        "filename": "microdroid/keymint/service.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 67,
      "author": {
        "id": 1132673
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-07-12T14:26:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "What do you mean by \u0027normal accessor\u0027? GetProperty in libbase doesn\u0027t cache the result. The result is created on the stack. \n\nhttps://source.corp.google.com/android/system/libbase/properties.cpp;rcl\u003d2f02239dd832effb52fa7a9b5109a764840a4978;l\u003d109\n\n\u003e Will all the processes have the secret mapped into their memory that they could access even if the SELinux rules say they can\u0027t?\n\nNo. If the process doesn\u0027t have a permission to read the sysprop, the file is not mapped to the process.",
      "parentUuid": "6a6a40d1_d92efebb",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 66,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 67,
        "endChar": 68
      },
      "revId": "8b2ea14ea9e914347d0922850834e9c671bd518c",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "faf89db1_644d3ce1",
        "filename": "microdroid/keymint/service.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 67,
      "author": {
        "id": 1139038
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-07-13T00:48:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "GetProperty returns a std::string so, IIUC, a piece of memory is allocated and the property value is copied into it. KeymasterBlob is almost the same, except the destructor actively zeros the allocated memory. Alternatively, we can copy it out of the string and explicitly wipe the string if that\u0027s the preferred API for properties.",
      "parentUuid": "f77fc38f_66987368",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 66,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 67,
        "endChar": 68
      },
      "revId": "8b2ea14ea9e914347d0922850834e9c671bd518c",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
